WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.930
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:10.429
Approximately 5 months ago, we talked about
a technique called Neural Radiance Fields,

00:00:10.429 --> 00:00:16.359
or NERF in short, where the input is the location
of the camera and an image of what the camera

00:00:16.359 --> 00:00:22.480
sees, we take a few of those, give them to
a neural network to learn them, and synthesize

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:28.640
new, previously unseen views of not just the
materials in the scene, but the entire scene

00:00:28.640 --> 00:00:29.640
itself.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:35.520
In short, we take a few samples, and the neural
network learns what should be there between

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:36.920
the samples.

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:43.010
In comes a non-continuous data, a bunch of
photos, and out goes a continuous video where

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.469
the AI fills in the data between these samples.

00:00:46.469 --> 00:00:51.699
With this, we can change the view direction,
but only that!

00:00:51.699 --> 00:00:54.899
This concept can also be used for other variables.

00:00:54.899 --> 00:00:59.699
For instance, this work is able to change
the lighting, but only the lighting.

00:00:59.699 --> 00:01:06.350
By the way, this is from long ago, from around
Two Minute Papers episode number 13, so our

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:11.600
seasoned Fellow Scholars know that this was
almost 500 episodes ago.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.610
Or, the third potential variable is time.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:20.560
With this AI-based physics simulator, we can
advance the time, and the algorithm would

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:24.860
try to guess how a piece of fluid would evolve
over time.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:31.540
This was amazing, but as you might have guessed,
we can advance time, but only the time.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:36.079
And this was just a couple of examples from
a slew of works that are capable of doing

00:01:36.079 --> 00:01:39.030
one, or at most, two of these.

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:44.330
These are all amazing techniques, but they
offer separate features.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:50.180
One can change the view, but nothing else,
one for the illumination, but nothing else,

00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:53.110
and one for time, but nothing else.

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:58.000
With the advent of neural network-based learning
algorithms, I wonder if it is possible to

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.530
create an algorithm that does all three?

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:03.830
Or is this just science fiction?

00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:09.130
Well, hold on to your papers, because with
this new work that goes by the name X-Fields,

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:16.080
we can indeed change the time…and the view
direction...and the lighting separately.

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:22.610
Or, even better, do all three at the same
time.

00:02:22.610 --> 00:02:23.640
Wo-hoo!

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:29.130
Look at how we can play with the time back
and forth and set the fluid levels as we desire,

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:35.120
that is the time part, and we can also play
with the other two parameters as well at the

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.230
same time.

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:42.290
But still, the results that we see here can
range from absolutely amazing, to trivial,

00:02:42.290 --> 00:02:45.240
depending on just one factor.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.600
And that factor is, how much training data
was available for the algorithm.

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:55.510
Neural networks typically require loads of
training data to learn a new concept.

00:02:55.510 --> 00:03:00.250
For instance, if we wish to teach to a neural
network what a cat is, we have to show it

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:03.400
thousands and thousands of images of cats.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:07.190
So, how much training data is needed for this?

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:13.620
And now, hold on to your papers, and…whoa…look
at these 5 dots here.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.780
Do you know what this means?

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:22.160
It means that all the AI saw was five images,
that is five samples from the scene with different

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:27.860
light positions, and it could fill in all
the missing details with such accuracy that

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.610
we can create this smooth and creamy transition.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:35.950
It almost feels like we have made at least
a 100 photographs of the scene.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:39.600
And all this from 5 input photos.

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:40.600
Absolutely amazing.

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.990
Now, here is my other favorite example.

00:03:43.990 --> 00:03:50.160
I am a light transport simulation researcher
by trade, so by definition, I love caustics.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:57.450
A caustic is a beautiful phenomenon in nature
where curved surfaces reflect or refract light,

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:00.540
and concentrate it to a relatively small area.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:05.450
I hope that you are not surprised when I say
that it is the favorite phenomenon of most

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:06.940
light transport researchers.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:11.040
And, just look at how beautifully it deals
with it.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:16.040
You could take any of these intermediate,
AI-generated images and sell them as real

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:19.110
ones and I doubt anyone would notice.

00:04:19.110 --> 00:04:25.100
So, it does three things that previous techniques
could do one by one, but really, how does

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:28.090
its quality compare to these previous methods?

00:04:28.090 --> 00:04:33.150
Let’s see how it does on thin geometry,
which is a notoriously difficult case for

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:34.570
these methods.

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:36.140
Here is a previous one.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:37.140
Look.

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:42.100
The thick part is reconstructed correctly,
however, look at the missing top of the grass

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:43.100
blade.

00:04:43.100 --> 00:04:45.010
Yup, that’s gone.

00:04:45.010 --> 00:04:49.750
A different previous technique by the name
Local Light Field Fusion not only missed the

00:04:49.750 --> 00:04:54.770
top as well, but also introduced halo-like
artifacts to the scene.

00:04:54.770 --> 00:05:00.320
And, as you see with this footage, the new
method solves all of these problems really

00:05:00.320 --> 00:05:06.580
well, and is quite close to the true reference
footage that we kept hidden from the AI.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:12.360
Perhaps the best part is that it also has
an online demo that you can try right now,

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:15.930
so make sure to click the link in the video
description to have a look.

00:05:15.930 --> 00:05:20.550
Of course, not even this technique is perfect,
there are cases where it might confuse the

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:25.670
foreground with the background, and we are
still not out of the water when it comes to

00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:26.980
thin geometry.

00:05:26.980 --> 00:05:32.230
Also, an extension that I would love to see
is changing material properties.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:37.250
Here, you see some results from our earlier
paper on neural rendering where we can change

00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:43.710
the material properties of this test object,
and get a near-perfect photorealistic image

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:47.520
of it in about 5 milliseconds per image.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.550
I would love to see it combined with a technique
like this one, and while it looks super challenging,

00:05:52.550 --> 00:05:57.870
it is easily possible that we will have something
like that within 2 years.

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:02.450
The link to our neural rendering paper and
its source code is also available in the video

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:03.790
description.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:58.020
What a time to be alive!

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:01.960
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

